Lung Abscess
Respiratory System
Foul-smelling sputum, Indolent fever/night sweats, Cavity with air-fluid level

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-028
Reason for Visit: Severe, progressive fatigue, drenching night sweats, and the new expectoration of foul-smelling brown sputum requiring urgent evaluation for a destructive lung infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with weeks of systemic decline who suddenly began coughing up large volumes of foul-smelling, putrid material and experiencing localized chest pain. Urgent assessment was clinically mandated to evaluate for a rupturing lung abscess and initiate targeted anaerobic antibiotic coverage before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I've been sweating through my sheets for weeks, but today I started coughing up mouthfuls of this disgusting, rotting-tasting brown phlegm.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been progressing indolently over the past 4 weeks. The patient reports severe, persistent fatigue, low-grade fevers, and drenching night sweats. Over the past 24 hours, the patient experienced a sudden, copious expectoration of foul-smelling, purulent sputum, likely representing the rupture of an abscess cavity into the bronchial tree. They note pleuritic chest pain directly over the right posterior lung segment and intermittent hemoptysis. There is a history of poor dentition and an episode of suspected alcohol-related decreased consciousness several weeks prior (aspiration risk). They report severe, unintentional weight loss.
Functional Status: Highly fatigued, spending the majority of the day in bed.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; history of alcohol misuse; poor nutritional status.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 38.3 C | RR: 22 breaths/min | SpO2: 94% on room air | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Cachectic, chronically ill appearance, fetid breath odor (halitosis) is immediately noticeable upon entering the room.
Physical Exam: Oral cavity reveals severe periodontitis and poor dentition. Chest examination reveals dullness to percussion over the right mid-to-lower posterior lung field. Auscultation demonstrates cavernous/amphoric breath sounds and coarse crackles over the consolidated area. Mild digital clubbing is present. The clinical picture strongly points to a cavitary lesion with an air-fluid level, consistent with a lung abscess.
Environmental Observations: Sputum mug at bedside contains dark, foul-smelling purulent material.
Medication Reconciliation: No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Lung Abscess - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pulmonary Tuberculosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The extremely foul-smelling nature of the sputum and the lower/mid-zone localization (TB typically favors apices), though it remains a vital differential requiring formal exclusion.

Bronchiectasis Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct single localized area of consolidation/cavernous breathing rather than bilateral basal crackles.

Simple Community-Acquired Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The indolent timeline (weeks), severe weight loss, and the sudden expectoration of putrid sputum.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic hallmarks of a lung abscess: an indolent course of fever and night sweats culminating in the sudden expectoration of foul-smelling sputum, occurring in a patient with poor dentition and a suspected aspiration event. The cavernous breath sounds and halitosis confirm severe, localized, necrotizing lung infection. Delaying care until the next in-hours period would allow unimpeded progression of tissue necrosis, risking massive hemoptysis or rupture into the pleural space (empyema). An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to confirm the focal necrotizing nature of the infection, initiate aggressive broad-spectrum antibiotics with anaerobic coverage, and arrange urgent imaging.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced high-dose Amoxicillin/Clavulanate (875/125mg twice daily) and Metronidazole (400mg three times daily) to cover oral anaerobes. Advised postural drainage positioning.
Positive Behavioral Change: Lie on your left side with your hips slightly elevated on pillows for 20 minutes twice a day; using gravity to position your healthy lung beneath your infected lung physically drains the rotting fluid out of the cavity, directly speeding up the clearance of the infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours to arrange an immediate chest X-ray or CT scan to visualize the cavity and air-fluid level.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer (call 000) to the Emergency Department if coughing produces a large volume of fresh red blood, if sudden and severe sharp chest pain occurs, or if breathing becomes rapid and difficult at rest.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis requires confirmation with a chest CT scan, and sputum cultures are necessary to rule out tuberculosis.
Clinical Course: A lung abscess requires a very long course of antibiotics, often 4 to 6 weeks, to fully eradicate the infection and heal the cavity.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you cough up more than a tablespoon of bright red blood, or if you suddenly feel unable to catch your breath.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Bacteria from your mouth accidentally went down into your lung weeks ago, slowly destroying a small pocket of lung tissue; the foul sputum means the pocket has burst open, and these two antibiotics are required to kill the specific deep-tissue bacteria.
Actionable Steps: You must take both antibiotics exactly as prescribed for the entire prolonged course; stopping early when you feel better will allow the bacteria deep inside the cavity to survive and destroy more lung tissue.

